From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 15:22:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01/22] H2a: cap object nesting at 256 in hayro-syntax, Type 4
 and PostScript parsers

hayro parses arrays and dictionaries recursively, so 200,000 levels of
nesting in a page dictionary, a resources dictionary or a content-stream
operand overflowed even an 8 MiB stack and aborted the process. The same
held for nested procedures in Type 4 (PostScript calculator) functions and
for `<<`/`>>` runs in hayro-postscript's scanner.

hayro-syntax: `Reader` tracks the current nesting depth; `Array::skip`,
`Dict::skip` and `parse_dict_with` (every way of reading a dictionary) refuse
to go deeper than `MAX_OBJECT_DEPTH` (256), so the object fails to parse like
any other malformed object. Each refusal bumps a thread-local counter,
`object_depth_limit_hits()`, so a caller can tell a nesting failure from
other parse failures by sampling it around an operation; content operands are
parsed with no document context, so a per-document counter could not see them.

hayro-interpret: `parse_procedure_inner` takes a depth and refuses beyond the
same cap, reporting through `note_object_depth_limit_hit()`.

hayro-postscript: `<<`/`>>` skipping and array nesting are capped at 256 and
fail with `Error::LimitCheck` (resolves the stack-overflow TODO).

Tests (crates/core/tests/h2a_object_depth.rs): the four 200,000-deep hostile
fixtures open and interpret on a 512 KiB stack, also in a child process (they
aborted before this change); the 256/257 boundary for dictionaries, arrays
and mixed nesting; per-thread counting; Type 4 and PostScript caps. The test
kit's expected page count for the over-deep page dictionary drops to 0.
